# Stage 1: example B1 with a neutron beam, everything P9-P14 owes inactivated,
# and Decay and hadElastic LEFT ACTIVE.
#
# THE ONE ROW THAT IS NOT A STAGE-1 CONFIGURATION, and it cannot be made one. Elastic,
# inelastic and capture are sub-processes of `NeutronGeneralProc`, whose
# EnableNeutronGeneralProcess flag `G4HadronInelasticQBBC::ConstructProcess` sets
# unconditionally with no messenger anywhere in 11.1.1 - so /process/inactivate takes all
# three or none. This macro takes all three, which is stage ZERO for a neutron: both sides
# deposit exactly nothing in the scoring volume, and that zero is a prediction rather than
# an absence. `Decay` is left active because a free neutron's 880 s lifetime makes it inert
# at any energy that crosses a phantom, and inactivating an inert process would make the
# macro differ from Geant4's default for no reason. See stage1_README.md and
# docs/RISK.md V53.
#
# Every name below is a row of ref/oracle/species_processes.csv for neutron - the dump of
# the constructed QBBC's own process manager. /process/inactivate ignores a name the
# species does not have, silently, so a macro written from the source rather than from
# the object can look like it switched something off. docs/RISK.md V43.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 1

/process/inactivate NeutronGeneralProc

# The stage, recorded by what RAN and not by what was intended. docs/RISK.md V43's
# lesson: ask the object. This prints Active/InActive for every process on the
# particle's manager, after the inactivations above.
/particle/select neutron
/particle/process/dump

/gun/particle neutron
/gun/energy 100 MeV

/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn 500000
